.TH ROOTFS 7 "March 2026" "Substrate" "Miscellaneous Information Manual"
.SH NAME
rootfs \- constructing and booting a Substrate root filesystem image
.SH DESCRIPTION
Substrate expects a conventional Unix root filesystem image containing at least
an init program, a shell, device directories, and the normal top-level
hierarchy.
.PP
The repository already carries a reference ext2 image at
.IR root.img .
That image can be used directly for QEMU bring-up or copied and modified for
experiments.
.SH STAGING A ROOT TREE
The top-level build installs a complete staged target tree into
.B dist/
by default.
Build and stage it with:
.PP
.nf
make
make install DESTDIR=$PWD/dist
.fi
.PP
The install step creates the standard root hierarchy, including
.BR /bin ,
.BR /sbin ,
.BR /lib ,
.BR /usr ,
.BR /tmp ,
.BR /dev ,
.BR /proc ,
and
.BR /sys .
.PP
The kernel mounts
.BR devfs ,
.BR procfs ,
and
.BR sysfs
itself after the root filesystem is established, but the mount points should
still exist in the image.
.SH BUILDING AN EXT2 ROOT IMAGE
Create a fresh image from the staged tree with normal host ext2 tools:
.PP
.nf
truncate -s 256M myroot.img
mkfs.ext2 -F myroot.img

tmpdir=$(mktemp -d)
sudo mount -o loop myroot.img "$tmpdir"
sudo cp -a dist/. "$tmpdir"/
sudo mkdir -p "$tmpdir"/dev "$tmpdir"/proc "$tmpdir"/sys "$tmpdir"/tmp
sudo chmod 1777 "$tmpdir"/tmp
sync
sudo umount "$tmpdir"
rmdir "$tmpdir"
.fi
.PP
The resulting image is suitable for use as a plain block-device root
filesystem.
.SH MODIFYING THE REFERENCE IMAGE
To use the in-repo reference image as a starting point:
.PP
.nf
cp root.img myroot.img
tmpdir=$(mktemp -d)
sudo mount -o loop myroot.img "$tmpdir"
\&...modify files...
sync
sudo umount "$tmpdir"
rmdir "$tmpdir"
.fi
.SH REQUIRED CONTENTS
At minimum, a bootable root image should provide:
.IP \(bu 2
an init program at
.BR /sbin/init ,
or an explicit
.B init=
override at boot
.IP \(bu
a working shell such as
.BR /bin/sh
.IP \(bu
directories
.BR /dev ,
.BR /proc ,
.BR /sys ,
and
.BR /tmp
.IP \(bu
the userland and libraries required by the selected init program
.SH QEMU BOOT EXAMPLES
Boot the reference ext2 image as the primary IDE disk:
.PP
.nf
qemu-system-i386 \\
    -kernel sys/kernel.bin \\
    -append "serial_debug root=/dev/storage/ide0 init=/bin/sh" \\
    -serial stdio \\
    -hda root.img \\
    -m 2048
.fi
.PP
Boot the BIOS
.B zImage
artifact directly:
.PP
.nf
qemu-system-i386 \\
    -kernel sys/kernel.zimage \\
    -append "serial_debug root=/dev/storage/ide0 init=/bin/sh" \\
    -serial stdio \\
    -hda root.img \\
    -m 2048
.fi
.PP
Boot the BIOS floppy image and use the default command line by pressing
.B Enter
at the loader prompt:
.PP
.nf
qemu-system-i386 \\
    -fda sys/kernel.flp \\
    -hda root.img \\
    -serial stdio \\
    -m 2048
.fi
.SH NOTES
The tracked
.I root.img
is a reference root filesystem, not a generated artifact.
It is intentionally kept at the repository root rather than under
.B dist/
because
.B dist/
is only the staged contents of a target filesystem tree.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kernel_command_line (7),
.BR execve (2),
.BR mount (2)
